    <h1>Hrafnsmál: The Raven's Speech and Harald Fairhair's Victory</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Hrafnsmál (9th century, attributed to Þorbjörn hornklofi) is a skaldic dialogue poem in which a raven reports Harald Fairhair's military victories to a valkyrie. The raven speaks as the authentic eyewitness of battle — a figure whose presence at carnage grants authoritative firsthand knowledge. This positions the raven as narrator and historical transmitter, extending its role beyond omen-bird into something closer to Huginn and Muninn's function as Odin's cosmic informants.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Poem's Structure and Setting</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Hrafnsmál survives in fragmentary form in the 13th-century compilation Fagrskinna (a kings' saga compilation) and in Snorri Sturluson's Heimskringla. The poem opens with a dialogue frame:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>A valkyrie (described as "shield-maid," wearing a helmet and holding a spear) meets a raven. The raven has just come from a battle, and its feathers are bloodstained — it has been feeding on the slain.</li>
      <li>The valkyrie asks where the raven has come from and what it knows of Harald's warriors. The raven then speaks at length, describing the king's retinue.</li>
      <li>The metre is fornyrðislag (the "old verse" measure), the same alliterative metre used in the Poetic Edda — giving the poem the same formal weight as mythological verse.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven as Battlefield Witness</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The choice of a raven as narrator is deliberate and significant within the Norse symbolic framework. The raven can serve as an authoritative narrator of battle for several reasons:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Physical presence:</strong> ravens are actual scavengers of battlefields — their appearance at a site of carnage is empirically predictable. The Norse poet uses this real behaviour as the foundation for the narrative conceit: the raven was there because ravens are always there.</li>
      <li><strong>Odinic association:</strong> as Odin's sacred birds, ravens carry authority in the Norse worldview. A raven's report is implicitly Odin-vouched — the god of battle has his bird watching every field.</li>
      <li><strong>Kenning tradition:</strong> the Norse skaldic tradition uses raven-kennings extensively for war and death (the raven-feeder = warrior; the raven's field = battlefield). Using a raven as narrator builds on this dense intertextual association — the poem's audience would recognise the raven as the proper perspective from which to view battle.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Warriors Described</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The raven's speech in Hrafnsmál describes Harald's warrior retinue in detail — providing one of the primary literary sources for Norse warrior types:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Berserkers (berserkir):</strong> the poem describes Harald's berserkers as wolf-skinned fighters who go into battle without armour, in a battle-fury (berserksgangr). The description in Hrafnsmál is one of the foundational texts for understanding the berserker phenomenon.</li>
      <li><strong>Ulfhéðnar:</strong> the wolf-coated warriors — a variant of the berserker tradition associated with wolves rather than bears. Hrafnsmál distinguishes them from berserkers.</li>
      <li><strong>Hirðmenn:</strong> the household warriors who form the king's standing retinue, described in terms of their loyalty, equipment, and skill.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The raven's description of these warriors is simultaneously a praise poem for Harald and a portrait of the ideal Norse warrior class as the raven — the battlefield's permanent resident — would know them.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Historical Context: Harald Fairhair</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Harald Fairhair (Harald Hårfagre, c.850–c.933) is the first king credited with unifying Norway into a single kingdom. The Battle of Hafrsfjord (c.872) — usually considered the decisive battle in his unification campaign — is the event Hrafnsmál most likely celebrates. As a contemporary skaldic composition (if the attribution to Þorbjörn hornklofi is correct), Hrafnsmál would be one of the earliest surviving poems composed about a specifically historical Norwegian king — making the raven's testimony a vehicle for historical record as much as poetic praise.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

